## 1.  Error handling

| Current | Problem | Fix |
|---------|---------|-----|
| No error‑handling middleware. | Unhandled errors crash the server or result in leaking stack traces to the client. | Add a middleware at the end of the routing stack. Use `next(err)` inside route handlers (wrap with async‑handler or try/catch). |
| No global `process.on('unhandledRejection')` guard. | Hanging Promises can silently fail. | Register a process‑wide guard to log and exit gracefully. |

### Example

```js
// errorHandler.js
const errorHandler = (err, req, res, next) => {
  console.error(err) // Consider using a logger instead of console

  // Don't expose stack traces in production
  const response = {
    message: err.message || 'Internal Server Error',
  }
  if (process.env.NODE_ENV !== 'production') {
    response.stack = err.stack
  }

  res.status(err.statusCode || 500).json(response)
}

module.exports = errorHandler
```

```js
// In app.js
const errorHandler = require('./errorHandler')

app.use('/health', healthRouter)     // or inline as before
app.use('/users', usersRouter)

app.use(errorHandler)               // <-- central error handler
```

If you later write an async route:

```js
app.get('/users', async (req, res, next) => {
  try {
    const users = await fetchUsers()
    res.json(users)
  } catch (err) {
    next(err)   // <‑‑ passes to the errorHandler
  }
})
```

